\begin{abstract}
We demonstrate that lead-lag conclusions in iterative model collapse are determined more by method choice than by the data: naive cross-correlation yields 100\% significance (16/16 metric pairs), while Toda--Yamamoto Granger causality retains only 25\% (4/16), with inter-method agreement of $\kappa = 0.185$. We identify three methodological pitfalls---method sensitivity, spurious regression under shared trends, and regime dependence---that together inflate spurious-signal rates to 62.5\% (75\% when including the underpowered Toda--Yamamoto test). To replace single-method reporting, we introduce a multi-method consensus framework requiring agreement across ${\geq}4$ of 5 statistical tests with FDR correction, achieving 0\% false-positive rate (single seed at $\alpha{=}0.0$) and 0\% false-negative rate on reference extreme regimes. Applied to Pythia-410M across 4 seeds, 5 synthetic-data mixing ratios ($\alpha$), and 10 iterative training generations on WikiText-103, the framework reveals a sharp regime boundary: significant lead-lag relationships emerge at $\alpha{=}1.0$ (with preliminary single-seed evidence at $\alpha{=}0.75$), where token entropy Granger-precedes diversity collapse with a one-generation lead ($|r| > 0.99$, 24/24 pairs significant, 0/24 in the reverse direction). Differencing eliminates all perplexity leads ($8/8 \to 0/8$) but preserves all diversity leads (24/24), confirming token entropy as a robust early-warning canary metric.
\end{abstract}

\section*{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

Several limitations constrain the generalizability of our findings.

\paragraph{Model scale.}
Our primary experiments use Pythia-410M (410M parameters); preliminary cross-architecture validation uses GPT-2-medium (345M parameters).
Both are small by contemporary standards.
Whether the entropy-leads-diversity pattern and the consensus framework's operating thresholds hold at billion-parameter scale remains untested.
The batch size difference between architectures (4 for Pythia vs.\ 32 for GPT-2-medium) means each training step exposes $8\times$ more data in the latter, potentially accelerating collapse dynamics without altering the qualitative pattern.

\paragraph{Data domain.}
Training data is drawn from a single domain (a WikiText-103 subset), and domain-specific distributional properties---vocabulary richness, syntactic complexity, topical diversity---may influence the coupling between canary and downstream metrics.
Domains with inherently low diversity (e.g., legal text, code) could exhibit different canary dynamics.

\paragraph{Short time series ($T{=}10$).}
With 11 data points per series, our statistical analyses operate near the lower bound of what standard methods require.
This limits power for all methods and makes the Toda--Yamamoto procedure particularly unreliable (power estimated at 0.05--0.20 for our effect sizes).
Longer generation chains would improve statistical power but impose substantial computational cost.

\paragraph{Bivariate Granger $\neq$ causality.}
Bivariate Granger tests cannot exclude confounders.
An unobserved process---such as a latent shift in the training data distribution---could independently drive both token entropy and diversity metrics, producing spurious lead-lag associations.
Our multi-method consensus framework mitigates but does not eliminate this limitation; multivariate VAR models would be required to test for confounders directly.

\paragraph{Regime boundary uncertainty.}
The detectability boundary at $\alpha{\approx}0.50$--$0.75$ is characterized with limited resolution (five $\alpha$ levels).
Whether this boundary is sharp (phase-transition-like) or gradual, and whether it shifts with model scale or data properties, remains open.
The $\alpha{=}0.75$ evidence is currently single-seed and awaits multi-seed confirmation.

\paragraph{No intervention experiment.}
We observe that token entropy Granger-precedes diversity collapse but do not test whether acting on canary signals (e.g., halting training or reducing $\alpha$) can prevent or delay collapse onset.
Without intervention evidence, the practical value of the canary remains theoretical.

\paragraph{Replace-only protocol.}
Our experiments use replace-only data accumulation; accumulate-mode training~\cite{dey2024breaking,kazdan2025collapse} may exhibit qualitatively different canary dynamics or eliminate the need for canary monitoring entirely~\cite{falahati2026curated}.

\paragraph{Scope limited to diversity metrics.}
Our canary claims are scoped to distinct-$n$ diversity metrics~\cite{li2016diversity}---not to model collapse broadly~\cite{schaeffer2025position}---as the perplexity channel proved spurious after differencing.
This scoping is theoretically motivated by the calibration-diversity tradeoff~\cite{cao2025entropycal}, but means our findings may not generalize to other collapse manifestations (e.g., factual degradation, reasoning capability loss).

\section*{Ethics Statement and AI Writing Disclosure}

\paragraph{AI writing assistance.}
This paper was drafted with assistance from Claude (Anthropic), used for LaTeX formatting, literature search, and iterative revision.
All scientific claims, experimental design decisions, data analysis, and interpretations are the sole responsibility of the authors.
The AI assistant did not design experiments, run code, or make editorial judgments about which results to include or exclude.

\paragraph{Responsible NLP considerations.}
This work studies degradation in iterative language model training.
We do not release models trained on synthetic data, as they exhibit reduced diversity and calibration.
Our findings highlight a failure mode relevant to the responsible deployment of language models: unmonitored iterative training on web-crawled data (which increasingly contains LLM outputs) may degrade model quality in ways detectable only through the multi-method framework we propose.
We encourage practitioners to implement canary monitoring in production training pipelines as a safeguard against silent quality degradation.
